@domenic [noted](https://github.com/w3ctag/design-reviews/issues/542#issuecomment-728276606)

> However a major point of contention remains around the integration with the web platform's module system (tc39/proposal-realms#261). In current implementations the module map is tied to "real" realms, which come with associated security principals, fetch clients, HTTP cache partitions, etc. These "synthetic" realms want to create their own module maps, which will require significant rearchitecting, in both spec and implementation.

I'd like to understand what this means. This PR contains the spec update. Are there more spec updates expected? Could you elaborate on the implementation concerns? I'd like to understand what the problem is with forwarding to the relevant principal Realm, with the associated properties you mention.

I'm not following something. ECMAScript doesn't have a notion of "principal" realm, so I'm not sure what kind of tracking ECMAScript would do there. There is in effect an incubator realm where the execution starts, but there is no fundamental reason that realm needs to stick around for ECMAScript.

```js
const realm = new ShadowRealm();
realm.evaluate(`(async () => {
  let i = 0;
  while (true) {
    await 0;
    print(`tick ${++i}`);
  }
})()`);
```

From what I understand, some built-in HTML operations rely on some state shared by a set of realms, with the incubator realm being considered principal (called "current" ?) and holding that state. If HTML needs to keep that structure, then it should be responsible for defining it.

I am still slightly concerned such an arrangement will create a risk of leaks from ShadowRealms onto the principal realm.

So I wrote a little script to go through (most of?) the web specs (based on [this list](https://github.com/w3c/browser-specs/blob/main/index.json), seems comprehensive as far as I can tell) and searched them for any use of "current realm".

<details>
<summary>Script source</summary>
    
```js
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { existsSync } from "node:fs";
import { mkdir, readFile, writeFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import specs from "./specs.json" assert { type: "json" };
import chalk from "chalk";

const specPages = new Set(specs.flatMap(spec => {
    const urls = [spec.url];
    if (spec.series?.releaseUrl) {
        urls.push(spec.series.releaseUrl);
    }
    if (spec.series?.nightlyUrl) {
        urls.push(spec.series.nightlyUrl);
    }
    if (spec.release?.url) {
        urls.push(spec.release.url);
    }
    if (spec.nightly?.url) {
        urls.push(spec.nightly.url);
    }
    if (spec.release?.pages) {
        urls.push(...spec.release.pages);
    }
    if (spec.nightly?.pages) {
        urls.push(...spec.nightly.pages);
    }

    return urls;
}));

async function fetchRetry(pageURL) {
    while (true) {
        try {
            const pageResponse = await fetch(pageURL);
            assert(pageResponse.headers.get("content-type", "text/html"));
            const pageText = await pageResponse.text();
            return pageText;
        } catch {
            await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 500));
        }
    }
}

const responsesCacheDir = new URL(`./responses/`, import.meta.url);
if (!existsSync(responsesCacheDir)) {
    await mkdir(responsesCacheDir);
}

async function loadCached(pageURL) {
    const cacheFile = new URL(
        `./responses/` + encodeURIComponent(pageURL.replaceAll("/", "-")),
        import.meta.url,
    );
    if (existsSync(cacheFile)) {
        return await readFile(cacheFile, 'utf8');
    }
    const pageText = await fetchRetry(pageURL);
    await writeFile(cacheFile, pageText);
    return pageText;
}


for (const pageURL of specPages) {
    const pageText = await loadCached(pageURL);
    if (pageText.toLowerCase().includes("current realm")) {
        console.log(chalk.blue(pageURL));
    }
}
```
    
</details>

From this there's only a few uses of "current realm" that either shouldn't be shadow realms, or is uncertain (other than HTML itself):

- https://w3c.github.io/webappsec-trusted-types/dist/spec/
    - I think this should look over all child shadow realms too, but I'm not sure
- https://webbluetoothcg.github.io/web-bluetooth/
    - Should be the principal realm (assuming bluetooth objects can't exist in shadow realms)
- https://wicg.github.io/performance-measure-memory/
    - Should be the principal realm
- https://w3c.github.io/permissions/
    - Should be the principal realm

All the other uses should create objects in the shadow realm (if the interfaces are (ever) exposed into Shadow Realms at all), or are in uses such as `StructuredDeserialize(..., current realm)` where again the shadow realm is the correct target not the principal realm.

@caridy I was under the impression from prior comments in the thread that the intent was to not expose TT in the Shadow. I’m not sure if this is a settled question, but it sounded like that was the intent.

Provisioning indirect eval is possible regardless. “Grandchild” ShadowRealm’s evaluate (and eval within it, etc) adds a rub because it means you’d have to virtualize each ShadowRealm’s ShadowRealm API so that a “child” at any depth is actually a “sibling”, i.e. a ShadowRealm created in the incubator realm. That’s doable too, accumulated bonus boundary-crossing taxes aside.

So with no TT in ShadowRealms and require-trusted-types-for enabled, the only thing which would not be possible afaict is for an incubator that is itself capable of direct eval to create an SR which shares that specific capability. This happens to matter in my case, which may just be bad luck; the scenario has some uniquely notable characteristics regardless, though.
